                <pb facs="0449" n="393" sameAs="frus:frus1876/pg393"/>

                <head>No. 210.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Foster to Mr. Fish.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1876-03-08"><hi rendition="#italic">March</hi> 8, 1876</date>.
                        (Received March 27, 1876.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 389.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have received a note from the acting
                    minister of foreign affairs, dated the 29th ultimo, of which I inclose a copy,
                    in which he informs me that his government has decided upon the removal of the
                    Indians now in the State of Chihuahua, from the Rio Grande border to Mapimi, a
                    point distant from the frontier, where they may be better guarded.</p>
                <p>Regarding this action as highly conducive to the peace of that frontier, in
                    acknowledging the receipt of Mr. Arias’ note, I took occasion to tender to his
                    government thanks for its resolution.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>JOHN W. FOSTER.</signed></closer>





